fix(api): enforce If-Match on matter memory and write it atomically

Three independent breaks in one chain, all now closed.

1. save_matter_memory took no headers at all, so If-Match was discarded and 409
   was unreachable however correct check_revision was. check_revision itself was
   DEAD CODE: defined at line 79, asserted by six unit tests, called from zero
   production sites. It is now called.

2. The revision is assigned server-side after the guard passes. The caller's
   If-Match is a claim about what it READ, never a value to store - trusting a
   body field would let a stale client reset the counter and defeat the guard on
   the next request.

3. std::fs::write truncates the target before writing, so a crash, OOM kill or
   full disk mid-write leaves a truncated or empty memory.json. That is
   unrecoverable: get_matter_memory cannot parse it and there is no backup. The
   guard is read BEFORE any file access, so a 409 leaves the file untouched - a
   conflict that had already truncated the file would be worse than no guard,
   because the caller would believe nothing was written.

A malformed If-Match is a 400, never a silent unconditional write: silently
ignoring a header the caller believes is protecting them is the worst outcome.

The wiring assertions read production source only, above '#[cfg(test)]' -
searching the whole file matches the tests' own string literals, which is how a
P4 assertion managed to fail against correct code.

Negative-tested: replacing the check_revision call with `let _ = expected` makes
the_save_handler_actually_reads_the_if_match_header fail with
'must CALL check_revision'. 62 lib tests pass; clippy clean.
